{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,86p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\nrg -n 'D721|D722|D725|D726|D728|D731|D734' docs/DECISIONS.md\nsed -n '11160,11210p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\nrg -n '\"'^##'\"' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\ntail -n 12 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Current state\n\n**Clean Steps 1–3, current (2026-11-11).** Records: Step 1, [round log](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§16–47; Step 2, [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§12–19.\n- **Step 1 configuration: c8, adopted by Alex (D387).** It runs DeepSeek Flash at high effort, with a 64,000-token output cap; a\n  format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380). It carries Alex's decisions D382–D385:\n  - figures are conditions;\n  - material statements with no thesis are kept without links;\n  - only big matters open a thesis;\n  - no target prices.\n\n  [PRD §12](../clean-arch/PRD.md) holds its system prompt, closing check and schema; a run with no candidate files sends exactly the\n  accepted request.\n- **Acceptance (D386, [protocol](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md)).** 50 fresh articles that no run,\n  old or new, had used: news from 2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts. One draw; two readers and a third on\n  disputes.\n  - helpful 132/139 (core 83/83);\n  - wrongly opened theses 1 of 10;\n  - search 141/143 available repeat pairs, or 141/145 for the whole system (one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs, and revisions are\n    barely exercised);\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57, plus 2 overlaps;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22. This is a storage comparison: every record keeps its own text. It is not a usability test.\n\n  Dev ×3 on the frozen golden-v5 (a lead): helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234; search 98.9–100%; clear wrong merges about 1%. Step 1\n  cost for the fresh set plus dev ×3: $4.20 at published rates.\n- **Steps 2 and 3** are built in isolation (`clean-arch/step2`, `clean-arch/step3`): search, comparison, grouping and later linking.\n  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) has not passed** on the clean path: its ladder step is built and\n  measured (four Stage A runs, lines missed by 1 to 3 answers; D426), so nothing yet shows the clean system can judge an investment\n  view on its own questions.\n- **Step 2 is closed and frozen (Alex, D427, 2026-11-11;** [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§14–19**).** It goes\n  to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines; `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` guards its files. Its three lines were met at the acceptance above (one draw; \"later version\"\n  relations barely exercised), and no miss of the seeded end-to-end test (clean 18, 18, 17 against production 17, 15, 16) has\n  been traced to it (the two single-draw misses were diagnosed on 2026-11-11, below: neither traces to Step 2). What happened since the close-out of 2026-11-08:\n  - A change to Jev's wording for \"later version\" relations failed its line (42 calls, 2026-11-09) and tuning stopped.\n  - One wrong relation was traced to the judge: it put NVIDIA's answer to the Kyber report under the FY2027 revenue question;\n    sent both ways, the conclusion did not differ ([result](../clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/RESULT.md)).\n  - Alex stopped work on that relation (D423) and asked for a fix to the rule that gives a \"later version\" the older record's\n    questions. Measured on the three saved draws ([round log](../clean-arch/step2-revision-links-2026-11-10/LOG.md)), removing the\n    rule was withdrawn: of the 24 links it wrote, two blind reader families call 19 context and 3 evidence, and one of the 3 is a\n    company's answer that would otherwise be missing beside four reports (with it rung 2, 2, 2; without it 2, 3, 2). **The rule\n    stays (D424).**\n  - **Open, recorded:** Jev's \"later version\" relation (9 of 30 join different statements in draw 1); the context links it carries\n    (19 of 24); wrong groups (at the line on one draw; the unprompted reading test never run); the \"Unverified quote\" mark not\n    shown to Jev (3 of 188); not decided, whether a company's answer must sit under every question its report is filed under.\n  - **Reopens when** Step 4 yields judged views on the clean path and a miss, or something a reader of a thesis sees, traces to\n    one of these. The round's measurement kit (replay of a saved draw, exact simulation of a writer rule, blind packets with\n    controls, a judgement sent both ways) is kept in the two folders above.\n- **The clean system's untraced end-to-end misses, diagnosed (2026-11-11;** [round log](../clean-arch/e2e-misses-2026-11-11/LOG.md)**).**\n  The China share question in draw 3 and the FY2028 revenue question in draw 2 (seeded test, D392). Both estimates sit on a rung\n  line. China: the same window-end request, re-sent, gives the miss twice and the right answer once (the judge's own variation at the\n  1% line); no H200 price is in any request. FY2028: draw 2's window-end request gives the miss 3 of 3 and draw 3's the right answer 3\n  of 3; draw 2's lacks records draw 3's holds (a \"41% in calendar 2027\" figure Step 1 filed under FY2027 only, \"$1 trillion of\n  Blackwell and Rubin visibility\", five infrastructure-demand records), not isolated which matters; FY2028 is the question whose filed\n  statements differ most between draws (47% shared, median 74%). Neither traces to Step 2. Refuted on the way: that a refused answer\n  re-asked caused the China miss. Observed defect of the frozen judge: it names the export-controls theme as a dependency of the China\n  question, 42 of 65 refusals in six draws, and the re-ask changed the rung 8 times (PRD 11, E4). No change proposed; 18 sends, $0.043.\n- **Open defects, by product impact** (reflection council, `council/av-codex.md`):\n  1. **The Ohio-guarantee yes/no question is never covered** in the three dev draws; a broader credit-support amount thesis opens\n     instead. Diagnosed (round log §48): the model folds the unsigned guarantee into the open aggregate thesis because the request\n     uses \"covers\" both for \"is evidence for\" and for \"already asks this question\". The same clause sits under link clutter.\n  2. **A revenue-share thesis was opened** (Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue), the class-A boundary of D382.\n  3. **Link clutter.** A blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful; the other 49 links were not rechecked.\n     The model's own account names topic or theme overlap and inference chains, and c8's closing check never reads back a link. This is\n     a likely cause, with no fix proven. Alex approved designing a protected link check through the council; a first draft was stopped\n     because it would drop founding premises and contrary facts.\n  4. **Unverified passages:** 3 of 188 fresh records and 1–3 per dev draw. The mark now travels into Step 2's records, and a reader sees the found source text or an \"Unverified quote\" label. Jev's comparison input still carries the model's text without the mark; changing it changes measured Step 2 behaviour and needs its own measurement.\n  5. **The lidar policy thesis** recurs at the boundary (one dev draw in three).\n- **History.** These earlier states are superseded:\n  - the 2026-11-07 takeover (K5 → K6b; [takeover log](../clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md));\n  - the 2026-11-08 close-out (K6b test 32/42; Step 2 first lines; [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12);\n  - the unfiled fix round and the c1–c7 arms (round log §§1–39).\n- **No run is live.** The branch is committed through the D388 line (round log §§48–59).\n\n**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n\nAlex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n\n**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n\n**Sealed (D379, 2026-11-06):** Alex asked to clean up and seal this version while he re-plans the system design. Work stops here and is sealed as it is: main's working tree is committed on branch `seal/2026-11-06` (tag `sealed-2026-11-06`) and merged into `main` on Alex's word (PR #10); the sealed split and two side experiments are pushed on their own branches (D379). No PRD 11 experiment is open, and the E1 next task below is withdrawn. The next task is Alex's re-planned system design.\n\n**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n\n**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n\n**Question-catalog limit removed (D377, 2026-11-05):** Alex also directed removal of the separate 150 KB catalog rejection. The research reader in this worktree now has neither the catalog nor evidence byte limit; it retains full scoped questions, historical boundaries, dependencies and evidence. A portable 326,996-byte catalog test returns all 400 new questions and expands evidence both after reading the catalog and directly; the old 150 KB guard fails the same test in a scratch copy. The saved 69-question pilot catalog and all 69 saved evidence expansions match their original content hashes. Details: `docs/research/2026-11-05-catalog-limit/`. The full suite remains pending until the paid seven-day run no longer competes for resources. The run remains in its isolated worktree and does not call this research reader.\n\n1055:| D711 | 2026-11-21 | Pilot pair 1 inconclusive (control-1 double refusal on 09-08: two one-character evidence-id copy errors); the comparison closed, no redraw or resume (council round 37, consensus A). Exploratory beside it: Data Center never above total revenue in linked-1 (control-1 28 of 60 by parser, sampled dates real); four linked CPU double counts in total revenue (H4); H1 at the line (5 against 1). Recommended to Alex: a citation-only judge version with closed per-request id lists, tested at $0; the link direction designed separately | in force (H4 reading corrected by D721) | `docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md` |\n1065:| D721 | 2026-11-22 | Council round 43/43b: the double-count premise is partly falsified by the frozen gold (q01 lists the Vera CPU target among the drivers above consensus; a sentence either way has no source); recommend closing the design with nothing built; D711's H4 corrected to \"four unsupported increments, overlap unresolved\" (only 07-15 differs in rung from the control); findings listed for Alex | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/codex-r43b.md` |\n1066:| D722 | 2026-11-22 | Alex closes the double-count design with nothing changed (\"收尾，不改 (推荐)\"): poc-v118 stays, no judge version opened, D721's findings recorded only; nothing queued | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1069:| D725 | 2026-11-22 | Council round 45/45b (Alex: find the design cause of unreliable numbers): related number questions have no shared, checked calculation — prose answers code cannot check, one question judged alone, split inputs; recommended: a new judge version judging related families in one call with labelled figures and identity/arithmetic checks, \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim, a $0 gate then a 36-call paid gate | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md` |\n1070:| D726 | 2026-11-22 | Alex opens a judge version for related number questions, $0 gate first (\"开，先做 0 元那一关 (推荐)\"); the paid gate needs his ceiling; first-release interim agreed: number questions show \"Forecast unavailable\" until they pass (\"同意 (推荐)\") | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1072:| D728 | 2026-11-22 | Alex: judging-v13 shows no earlier forecast in the calculation request (\"算的时候不给看 (推荐)\"); changed is derived by code afterwards; the evidence in view stays D198's; judging-v7 unchanged | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1075:| D731 | 2026-11-22 | judging-v13 paid-gate harness ready (council 47h): first attempt scored on both arms, retries diagnostic; packets hold the scored answers only and commit to their mapping; one-to-one reconciliation; sealed readings, one verdict; rehearsal 63/63, mutants 33/33; 36 first attempts ~$0.17 (≤$0.39); next: Alex names the ceiling (proposed $0.50) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md` |\n1078:| D734 | 2026-11-22 | Alex: one bounded repair of judging-v13's request and checks, then a registered $0 blind conformance test (\"修一次再零成本盲测 (推荐)\"); a clean result returns to him for a re-send of the same 36 requests | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\n1079:| D735 | 2026-11-22 | Council 49–50: the v13 repair is finished to a line (at most two more review rounds: wording and cases, then the sent request and checks; then the blind test once; a serious issue after the second goes back to Alex); shrinking to six checks set aside (drops C8's segment-into-total guard; exceeds D734); design v2 | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md` |\n    - Correct examples and planted defects are fixed first. An independent writer sees only the new request, and their first\n      outputs go through the real harness unedited.\n    - The test stops if a correct form is refused, a planted defect passes, or the writer needs an unstated rule.\n    - Only then is a re-send of the same 36 requests asked for, under a new decision with D725's lines unchanged.\n  - Claude's lean, a code-built catalogue of source expressions, was set aside: parser omissions would become missing evidence, and\n    the catalogue leaves the outcome link untouched.\n  - The alternative is to close judging-v13. poc-v118 stays the product (D690), and the number answers stay a known defect.\n- **Next:** Alex chooses between the repair and closing. Nothing is changed or sent before his answer.\n\n## D734 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: repair judging-v13's request and checks once, then a $0 blind conformance test\n\n- **The question** (after D733): \"判数字的新版本（一次判一组相关的数）花钱那一关没过：72 个答案全被挡在“填格式”这一步。原因是填写规则只写在检查代码里、没写进给模型的说明，而且检查代码自己也会把对的判错。这没回答“一起判会不会更准”。下一步怎么走？\"\n  Options:\n  - 修一次再零成本盲测 (recommended);\n  - 关掉这条线.\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"修一次再零成本盲测 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** one bounded repair of judging-v13's request and checks, per council round 48 (D733).\n  - Each field's rule goes beside its field, with enums where the schema can hold them.\n  - The demonstrated check failures are fixed, and the checks for scope changes, unstated assumptions and cycles are kept.\n  - Then a registered $0 blind conformance test, with its stop conditions. Nothing is paid or sent.\n  - A clean result goes back to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests under a new decision, with D725's lines\n    unchanged. A stop goes back to him with its census.\n- **The order of the work (AGENTS §2, §7.3):** the repair design with its tables, then council rounds until one finds nothing\n  serious, then code with tests and mutants. The test is registered before it runs, with its examples fixed first.\n\n## D735 · 2026-11-22 · Council rounds 49–50: the repair is finished to a line; at most two more review rounds, then the blind test\n\n- **Round 49** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r49.md`) reviewed the repair design v1 and found it not ready:\n  - rules unstated across C1–C10;\n  - two unsound check changes, a growth label the answer supplies itself, and containment that flips signs and negations;\n  - wrong test inputs (old-2 v41 is a Fed question; old-3 v6 is CY2026);\n  - a blind test that could pass with no usable answer.\n- **Round 50** (`claude-r49.md`, `codex-r50.md`):\n  - Claude argued that the repair is unbounded and that the format should shrink to six core checks, which goes back to Alex.\n  - Codex answered that the six drop C8's guard against adding a segment into its total, a defect D725 named. Shrinking changes the\n    design and exceeds D734.\n  - Consensus: finish D734's repair to a line. One round reviews the exact wording and cases, one reviews the request the harness\n    actually sends and its checks, then the blind test runs once. A serious issue left after the second round goes back to Alex.\n  - Round 50's size estimate: the largest schema grows from about 10,000 to 16,000–20,000 characters, at 13 to 22 figures an answer.\n    Only the paid re-send measures whether luna can follow it.\n- **Design v2** (`REPAIR-DESIGN.md`):\n  - the exact wording;\n  - markers for outcome and reference;\n  - enums for passage ids and units;\n  - the growth-label and containment changes dropped;\n  - extractor fixes for sign, trailing qualifiers and negation;\n  - signed period sets;\n  - corrected inputs: a fresh pair old-1 8810/8816 and S old-3 9043 at CY2026, with O and E as controls;\n  - stop conditions that require usable answers.\n12:## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n25:## 1. Goal (end to end)\n31:## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n57:## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n70:## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n93:## 5. What to do\n120:## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n168:## 7. Process (no drift)\n179:## 8. Steps and where we are\n193:## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n| 30 | 11-05 | Alex：\"你充分验证\"。乙的全部 22 道题验证：设计（`baseline-design.md` 第 8 轮）——文件由代码选（开始跟踪前最近一个财报日的财报类材料：5 月 20 日新闻稿和 CFO 说明）；读法和第二版一字不差，每题每份文件 3 次；判断照 PRD 10 §4 当天原样重发：三遍各自拿窗口末最后一次判断的存档请求，对照原样 3 次，起点组只在证据块最前面加上读到的数；过关三条事先写下（v10、v12 各 ≥ 6/9；不弄坏：同一遍里重排的精确检验显著少、或少 2 次以上且两人读理由认定是起点数带偏；读到的数五类分类，读错、别的量、不是数一个都不能有） | 发之前修了两处：读法的检查原来要求\"引用的最后一个数就是读到的数\"，照抄整句的正常引用会被拒（4 句全拒），改成\"读到的数印在引用里\"（对照 32 例全对，两个弄坏的版本分别抓出 5、1 个错；试点重放只有 v10 第 3 次那两条整行变成收下，v10 变 3/3）；发送脚本按冻结尺子补上是非题判法、\"档动了\"对着请求里那次上一次结论查（三遍存档 57 个结论和 `score_judgement.py` 逐题一致，弄坏的判法被抓出）。审查：Codex 4 轮（第 1 轮 5 条严重、第 2 轮 3 条严重，都属实都改；第 3、4 轮无严重问题），独立读者 1 次（请求干净、没有金标泄漏；挪档看不见、单位后缀、口径错等照改） | 设计和 8 个脚本锁定（`baseline-all.sha256`）；发读数 132 次 |\n| 31 | 11-05 | 乙的全部 22 道题验证，照锁定的设计测一次：读 132 次（0.083 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、判断 144 次（0.344 美元），三遍各自和当天原样重发的对照比 | **第 1 条过**：v10、v12 各 9/9 判对。**第 2 条过**：读到数的 7 道判过的题两组选档逐次一样，只有 v22 第 2 遍少 1 次（7/9 对 6/9，理由显示是本来的抽样起伏，起点组写明\"没有把一季度乘 4\"）。**第 3 条没过**：53 个收下的数里 5 个是别的量——v19（Vera CPU 收入）两次收了\"数据中心计算收入 604 亿\"，v20（Blackwell 和 Rubin 收入）两次收了\"数据中心收入 752 亿\"和\"网络收入 148 亿\"；判断都没被带偏（v19 理由写明计算收入里没有单列 Vera CPU） | 照登记停，交 Alex |\n| 32 | 11-05 | 问模型自己为什么收那 5 个数（3 次，约 0.3 美分，`probe-baseline-account.md`） | 3 次都指向读法任务里那一句\"报告没用题目的名字写这个量时，写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"，并自己说这些数不是这道题的量、不该写。这一句是 v10、v12 要用的，在报告根本没有这个量的题上把模型推去写更大或旁边的行 | 建议：只改这一句的边界（只收\"由它算出的那几行\"和\"换了名字的同一个量\"，更大的、旁边的行不算），按提示词审计改，再在 22 道题上测一次；等 Alex 的话 |\n| 33 | 11-05 | Alex：\"改\"。只改读法里那一句：原句\"写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"→ 新句\"只写算出它的那几行，或换了名字的同一个量；更大的行、旁边的行都不写\"（任务 790 → 889 字符，理由：抽象说法换成闭合两类加边界）。提示词审计、Codex 只读审 1 轮（1 条严重：发送失败会被当成白卷，已改成\"不完整、不下结论\"）；先在 v19、v20、v10、v12 上试发 24 次（1.6 美分） | 试发过：v19、v20 三次都白卷，v10 3/3；v12 2/3——问模型（2 次）：新句的\"更大的行\"半句让它把\"购置固定资产和无形资产\"当成比资本开支宽的一行，其中一次重想后改口\"就是资本开支\" | 照登记测全部 22 道题 |\n| 34 | 11-05 | 第三版的登记测试：和第 8 轮一样，只换这一句；读 132 次（0.078 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、读到数的 7 道题三遍重判、当天对照（108 次，0.268 美元） | **三条全过**：v10、v12 各 9/9；判对的题一道没变错（只有 v22 第 2 遍反过来对照 0/3、起点组 3/3，理由是在 41% 和 32% 两个增长估计间摇摆，和第 13 轮方向相反，与起点数无关）；46 个数全是自己的量（2 条是标明的二季度指引），两人分类全一致；v19、v20 的错数全没了，其余 15 道白卷、没冒出新错。没盖住：资本开支那一行试发时漏过 1 次；指引也被写成\"事实\"；一路判下来的效果和跨题效果要端到端才量得到 | 交 Alex：要不要做进生产、跑 14 天三遍端到端 |\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,sqlite3,collections,re,hashlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nbase=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers'\\\\'')\nsys.path.insert(0,str(base))\nimport paid_gate as pg\nplan_file=base/'\\\\''paid-plan.json'\\\\''\nrun=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nplan=json.loads(plan_file.read_bytes())\nprint('\\\\''plan hash'\\\\'', hashlib.sha256(plan_file.read_bytes()).hexdigest(), '\\\\''identical pinned run plan'\\\\'', plan_file.read_bytes()==(run/'\\\\''plan.json'\\\\'').read_bytes())\nquotes={passage['\\\\''text'\\\\''] for it in plan for passage in it['\\\\''passages'\\\\''].values()}\ncases=[('\\\\''P'\\\\'','\\\\''old-1'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v4'\\\\'',8810),('\\\\''v8'\\\\'',8816)]),('\\\\''S'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v6'\\\\'',9043)]),('\\\\''O'\\\\'','\\\\''old-2'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v29'\\\\'',8507)]),('\\\\''E'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v41'\\\\'',9071)])]\nfor name,draw,calls in cases:\n path=pg.RUN/draw/'\\\\''prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\''\n a=sqlite3.connect(f'\\\\''file:{path}?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',name,draw)\n for v,id in calls:\n  req=json.loads(a.execute('\\\\''select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(id,)).fetchone()[0])\n  print(id, pg.rv.parts(req['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''content'\\\\''])[0])\n  print('\\\\''database'\\\\'', pg.questions(draw,[v])[v],pg.bands(draw,v))\n user, ids,_=pg.rv.render(path,calls,{'\\\\''members'\\\\'':{},'\\\\''relations'\\\\'':[],'\\\\''shared'\\\\'':[]})\n passages=pg.passages(draw,ids)\n print('\\\\''items'\\\\'',len(ids),'\\\\''absent occurrences'\\\\'',sum(p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] not in quotes for p in passages.values()),'\\\\''absent unique'\\\\'',len({p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] for p in passages.values()}-quotes))\n for e,p in passages.items():\n  q=p['\\\\''text'\\\\'']\n  if (name=='\\\\''O'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$(?:18|25)(?:'\"\\\\.\\\\d+)?\\\\b'\\\\'',q)) or (name=='\\\\''E'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''double|20 billion|\\\\\"'$20B'\"'\\\\'',q,re.I)) or (name=='\\\\''S'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''730|800|1\\\\.3 trillion|four (?:largest|major)|four hyperscalers'\\\\'',q,re.I)):\n   print(e,p)\n if name=='\\\\''P'\\\\'':\n  for e,p in passages.items():\n   if p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] not in quotes and re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$['\"\\\\d.]+\\\\s*(?:billion|B|trillion)|[\\\\d.]+%'\\\\'',p['\\\\''text'\\\\'']) and re.search(r'\\\\''NVIDIA|NVDA|data center'\\\\'',p['\\\\''text'\\\\''],re.I):\n    print('\\\\''fresh'\\\\'',e,p['\\\\''text'\\\\''])\nfor j in ('\\\\''answers.jsonl'\\\\'','\\\\''retries.jsonl'\\\\''):\n rows=[r for l in (run/j).read_text().split('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'') if l and (r:=json.loads(l)).get('\\\\''data'\\\\'')]\n print(j,len(rows),collections.Counter(code for r in rows for code,d in r['\\\\''errors'\\\\'']))\n for r in rows:\n  for f in r['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n   if f['\\\\''operation'\\\\'']=='\\\\''difference'\\\\'' and len(f['\\\\''inputs'\\\\''] or [])>2:\n    print('\\\\''difference'\\\\'',r['\\\\''unit'\\\\''],r['\\\\''arm'\\\\''],r['\\\\''draw'\\\\''],f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''inputs'\\\\''],f['\\\\''signs'\\\\''])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,sqlite3,collections,re,hashlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nbase=Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers'\\\\'')\nsys.path.insert(0,str(base))\nimport paid_gate as pg\nplan_file=base/'\\\\''paid-plan.json'\\\\''\nrun=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nplan=json.loads(plan_file.read_bytes())\nprint('\\\\''plan hash'\\\\'', hashlib.sha256(plan_file.read_bytes()).hexdigest(), '\\\\''identical pinned run plan'\\\\'', plan_file.read_bytes()==(run/'\\\\''plan.json'\\\\'').read_bytes())\nquotes={passage['\\\\''text'\\\\''] for it in plan for passage in it['\\\\''passages'\\\\''].values()}\ncases=[('\\\\''P'\\\\'','\\\\''old-1'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v4'\\\\'',8810),('\\\\''v8'\\\\'',8816)]),('\\\\''S'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v6'\\\\'',9043)]),('\\\\''O'\\\\'','\\\\''old-2'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v29'\\\\'',8507)]),('\\\\''E'\\\\'','\\\\''old-3'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v41'\\\\'',9071)])]\nfor name,draw,calls in cases:\n path=pg.RUN/draw/'\\\\''prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\''\n a=sqlite3.connect(f'\\\\''file:{path}?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',name,draw)\n for v,id in calls:\n  req=json.loads(a.execute('\\\\''select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(id,)).fetchone()[0])\n  print(id, pg.rv.parts(req['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][1]['\\\\''content'\\\\''])[0])\n  print('\\\\''database'\\\\'', pg.questions(draw,[v])[v],pg.bands(draw,v))\n user, ids,_=pg.rv.render(path,calls,{'\\\\''members'\\\\'':{},'\\\\''relations'\\\\'':[],'\\\\''shared'\\\\'':[]})\n passages=pg.passages(draw,ids)\n print('\\\\''items'\\\\'',len(ids),'\\\\''absent occurrences'\\\\'',sum(p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] not in quotes for p in passages.values()),'\\\\''absent unique'\\\\'',len({p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] for p in passages.values()}-quotes))\n for e,p in passages.items():\n  q=p['\\\\''text'\\\\'']\n  if (name=='\\\\''O'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$(?:18|25)(?:'\"\\\\.\\\\d+)?\\\\b'\\\\'',q)) or (name=='\\\\''E'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''double|20 billion|\\\\\"'$20B'\"'\\\\'',q,re.I)) or (name=='\\\\''S'\\\\'' and re.search(r'\\\\''730|800|1\\\\.3 trillion|four (?:largest|major)|four hyperscalers'\\\\'',q,re.I)):\n   print(e,p)\n if name=='\\\\''P'\\\\'':\n  for e,p in passages.items():\n   if p['\\\\''text'\\\\''] not in quotes and re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$['\"\\\\d.]+\\\\s*(?:billion|B|trillion)|[\\\\d.]+%'\\\\'',p['\\\\''text'\\\\'']) and re.search(r'\\\\''NVIDIA|NVDA|data center'\\\\'',p['\\\\''text'\\\\''],re.I):\n    print('\\\\''fresh'\\\\'',e,p['\\\\''text'\\\\''])\nfor j in ('\\\\''answers.jsonl'\\\\'','\\\\''retries.jsonl'\\\\''):\n rows=[r for l in (run/j).read_text().split('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'') if l and (r:=json.loads(l)).get('\\\\''data'\\\\'')]\n print(j,len(rows),collections.Counter(code for r in rows for code,d in r['\\\\''errors'\\\\'']))\n for r in rows:\n  for f in r['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n   if f['\\\\''operation'\\\\'']=='\\\\''difference'\\\\'' and len(f['\\\\''inputs'\\\\''] or [])>2:\n    print('\\\\''difference'\\\\'',r['\\\\''unit'\\\\''],r['\\\\''arm'\\\\''],r['\\\\''draw'\\\\''],f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''inputs'\\\\''],f['\\\\''signs'\\\\''])'\"","aggregated_output":"plan hash 6f81dae5bd76debae944002f9a1421cb094cb05bbb36b4f4545bcb251db28478 identical pinned run plan False\n\n P old-1\n8810 Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\ndatabase {'question': \"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\", 'ladder': ['Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion.', 'Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.', 'Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion.', 'Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion.', 'Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.']} [[431.2, None], [392.0, 431.2], [352.8, 392.0], [313.6, 352.8], [None, 313.6]]\n8816 Thesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.\ndatabase {'question': 'How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?', 'ladder': ['FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion.', 'FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion.', 'FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion.', 'FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion.', 'FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.']} [[400.0, None], [350.0, 400.0], [300.0, 350.0], [250.0, 300.0], [None, 250.0]]\nitems 296 absent occurrences 79 absent unique 79\nfresh e4896 NVIDIA's revenues reached $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, representing a 106% year-over-year increase and an 18% rise sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\nfresh e4897 Data Center revenues came in at $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% from the previous quarter.\nfresh e4899 Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\nfresh e4906 The AI boom continues to benefit NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) as the firm grew its revenue by 106% annually during the second quarter.\nfresh e4907 the data center business accounting for $83.7 billion out of the $96.22 billion of the total revenue.\nfresh e4914 According to the latest Steam Hardware Survey, Nvidia GPUs still make up for 72.88% of Steam's player base.\nfresh e4926 Data center revenue rose to $89 billion\nfresh e4949 In late August, Anthropic signed a US$35 billion cloud-computing contract with Lambda, a cloud provider backed by NVIDIA.\nfresh e4950 Earlier in August, Anthropic executed a similar US$45 billion agreement with another NVIDIA-backed cloud provider, Nscale, to lease capacity in West Virginia.\nfresh e4962 Again. Just three weeks ago, Nvidia reported its fiscal second-quarter financial results. The company posted adjusted EPS of $2.22 on revenue of $96.22 billion. That beat Wall Street by more than $4.0 billion and was good for year-over-year growth of 106%.\nfresh e4975 Nvidia's adjusted revenue grew 106% year over year, while diluted earnings per share (EPS) rocketed 120%.\nfresh e4976 Nvidia, as it typically does, also knocked it out of the park with its guidance. The company forecast revenue of $108 billion in the current quarter, roughly $2.5 billion above estimates.\nfresh e4981 Nvidia generated $89 billion in AI chip revenue last quarter, compared with Broadcom's nearly $21 billion.\nfresh e4985 During the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the data center business generated $89.02 billion in revenues, accounting for more than 92% of total sales.\nfresh e4993 Even as Data Center revenue surged 117% year-over-year to $89 billion last quarter, Nvidia is now pushing to broaden who consumes its compute, not just who buys its GPUs.\nfresh e5003 The Data Center business remained the key growth driver, generating $89 billion in revenues, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially.\nfresh e5023 Nvidia controlled 90% of the discrete GPU market in the second quarter of 2026, according to Jon Peddie Research, while AMD held an 8% share.\nfresh e5024 In Nvidia's latest quarter, its data center revenue surged 117% year over year and accounted 92% of its top line.\nfresh e5025 In AMD's latest quarter, its data center revenue -- which includes both its Instinct GPUs and Epyc CPUs -- rose 107% but only accounted for 58% of its top line.\nfresh e5032 Nvidia's latest quarter makes the size of that exposure clear: data-center revenue reached $89 billion, representing about 92.5% of total quarterly sales, while management is targeting approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue.\nfresh e5044 In this framework, more than $302.97 billion in annual revenue and $192.88 billion in net income are tied back to an ecosystem that spans data center infrastructure, gaming graphics, and automotive compute rather than a single product cycle.\nfresh e5045 Data Center Revenue: Now more than 70% of total revenue and growing rapidly.\nfresh e5047 Nvidia generated $96.2 billion of revenue in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, up 106% year-over-year (YoY), while data center revenue reached $89 billion, up 117%.\nfresh e5052 Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) expects third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\nfresh e5057 In the second quarter of its fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026), Nvidia's hyperscaler revenue increased 13% sequentially to nearly $49 billion.\nfresh e5060 ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue.\nfresh e5065 Nvidia estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure will increase from about $25 billion with Blackwell systems to $40 billion with Vera Rubin systems, partly because the newer platform includes more CPUs, networking products, and other system components.\n\n S old-3\n9043 Thesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\ndatabase {'question': 'How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?', 'ladder': ['Combined spending is below $500 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion.', 'Combined spending is at least $800 billion.']} [[None, 500.0], [500.0, 600.0], [600.0, 700.0], [700.0, 800.0], [800.0, None]]\nitems 50 absent occurrences 41 absent unique 41\ne1856 {'text': 'Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion', 'speaker': '247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo'}\ne1947 {'text': 'hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', 'speaker': 'fool.com (Patrick Sanders)'}\ne2458 {'text': 'The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne2759 {'text': 'With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.', 'speaker': 'Colette Kress'}\ne4514 {'text': 'the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.', 'speaker': 'Jon Quast'}\ne4564 {'text': 'The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne4766 {'text': 'The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne5081 {'text': 'The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.', 'speaker': 'barchart.com (analyst)'}\ne5105 {'text': 'Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne5114 {'text': 'top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026', 'speaker': 'finance.yahoo.com author'}\ne5358 {'text': 'Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (Harsh Chauhan)'}\ne5376 {'text': 'Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\n\n O old-2\n8507 Thesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\ndatabase {'question': \"How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\", 'ladder': ['Commitments are below $25 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion.', 'Commitments are at least $100 billion.']} [[None, 25.0], [25.0, 50.0], [50.0, 75.0], [75.0, 100.0], [100.0, None]]\nitems 30 absent occurrences 0 absent unique 0\ne2620 {'text': 'These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.', 'speaker': 'NVIDIA Corporation'}\ne2889 {'text': 'The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.', 'speaker': \"Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement\"}\ne3917 {'text': 'The company also reported a further $25 billion in equity investment commitments as of July 26.', 'speaker': \"Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's latest earnings report\"}\ne4053 {'text': 'The company reported that, as of the end of the quarter, it had about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies, as well as another $25 billion in commitments for future investments.', 'speaker': 'calcalistech.com'}\n\n E old-3\n9071 Thesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\ndatabase {'question': 'What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?', 'ladder': ['FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion.', 'FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.']} [[50.0, None], [40.0, 50.0], [30.0, 40.0], [20.0, 30.0], [None, 20.0]]\nitems 15 absent occurrences 0 absent unique 0\ne3660 {'text': 'The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.', 'speaker': \"Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement\"}\ne4687 {'text': \"AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\", 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne4691 {'text': \"What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\", 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\ne4764 {'text': 'Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year', 'speaker': 'fool.com (Keith Noonan)'}\ne4856 {'text': 'with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.', 'speaker': 'fool.com (analyst)'}\nanswers.jsonl 36 Counter({'C7': 99, 'C1': 11})\ndifference pair-old-2 separate-v9 1 total_fy27_q4_implied ['total_fy27_consensus', 'total_fy27_q1_reported', 'total_fy27_q2_reported', 'total_fy27_q3_guidance'] [1, -1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-3 joint 1 total_fy26_q4_implied ['total_fy26', 'total_fy26_h1', 'total_fy26_q3_implied'] [1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-1 separate-v8 2 dc_q4_fy27_forecast ['dc_fy27_forecast', 'dc_h1_fy27', 'dc_q3_fy27_estimate'] [1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-3 separate-v3 2 f10 ['f1', 'f2', 'f9'] [1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-3 separate-v35 2 total_q4_fy2027 ['total_fy2027_consensus', 'total_h1_fy2027', 'total_q3_guidance'] [1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-1 joint 3 fy26_q4_total ['fy26_total', 'fy26_q1_total', 'fy26_q2_total', 'fy26_q3_total'] [1, -1, -1, -1]\ndifference pair-old-1 separate-v8 3 f8 ['f4', 'f5', 'f6', 'f7'] [1, -1, -1, -1]\nretries.jsonl 36 Counter({'C7': 93, 'C1': 32, 'C8': 16, 'C2': 7})\ndifference pair-old-1 joint 1 fy2026_q4_base_est ['fy2026_total', 'fy2026_q1_base_est', 'fy2026_q2_base_est', 'fy2026_q3_base'] [1, -1, -1, -1]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
