{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\\\"'$1'\"\\\\.2 trillion|\\\\\"'$500 million|7'\"\\\\.5%|\\\\\"'$4'\"\\\\.5 billion|\\\\\"'$3–4 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$300 million|'\"\\\\\"'$5 billion|about 40%|more than '\"\\\\\"'$2 billion|more than double|more than triple'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{gate.py,DESIGN.md,REQUEST-AUDIT.md,REPAIR-DESIGN.md}\nrg -n 'def check_schema|def _check' src/hivemind/model.py\nrg -n 'check.*answer|notes|cycle|cancel' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:71:| **C1 source expression** [46b.2] | code extracts every complete numeric expression from the cited passage as shown, with its qualifiers and unit. Points: \"$108.0 billion\". Ranges: \"$105–110 billion\". Relative: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" is midpoint 108 ±2%. Bounds: \"more than $730 billion\" (>730), \"at least\" (>=). Approximate bounds: \"nearly $800 billion\" (approx_below 800). Multipliers: \"double\" ×2, \"more than double\" (>×2). A source figure names one extracted expression and a component of it (midpoint, low, high, bound, multiplier), and must equal it exactly: value, unit, sign and qualifier. Any other wording cannot be a source figure | a figure that strips a qualifier ($730 from \"more than $730 billion\"); a bound used as a point; a wrong number within 0.5% (108.5); a figure naming no extracted expression |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:85:| **C2 bound direction** [46f.3] | for positive operands, a result's bound side follows its inputs: sums and products keep a lower or upper side, a ratio's or difference's second operand flips it, and mixed sides give `approx` | \"more than double\" times $20B declared below $40B |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:161:| R4 | old-3 9166: 20 × (>2) (e3660, \"more than double\") gives >40, trimmed to 38–39 by `adjusts: trim` | recorded adjustment that definitely crosses the $40B boundary (rung 2 is $40–50B, rung 1 $50B+) |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:68:| `unit` | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). USD: a price per unit in dollars. %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5; a fall of 12% = -12). x: a multiplier (double = 2). units: a count. An outcome is in its member's unit (USD bn).\" |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:70:| `expression` | \"Source only: copied word for word from that passage's Quote line, never its summary: the qualifier, the number and its scale, and nothing else, for example 'more than $2 billion', 'about 40%', '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%', '$3–4 billion', '$300 million or more', 'more than triple'. Qualifiers: more than, over, at least, nearly, almost, approximately, about, around, roughly, up to, less than, below, under, and 'or more' or 'or less' after the number. A negated figure ('will not exceed $5 billion') cannot be a source; state it as an estimate.\" |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:71:| `component` | \"Source only, which part of the expression the figure takes. value: a plain amount or a percentage ('$2 billion'; 'about 40%' is value 40 with bound approx). bound: a qualified amount ('more than $2 billion' is value 2 with bound '>'). low and high: one figure for each end of a written range, each with value. midpoint and relative: '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' is two figures, 4.5 USD bn and 3 %. multiplier: 'double' or 'triple' (value 2 or 3, unit x; 'more than double' adds bound '>').\" |\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:182:  - \"$300 million or more\" taken as a point (C1);\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REQUEST-AUDIT.md:44:  example 'more than $2 billion', '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%', 'more than triple')\". The examples come from no gate case [46f.4].\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:185:        src('m2', 'e3660', 'more than double', 2.0, 'x', 'guidance', W('NVIDIA', 'revenue multiple', 'CPU', 'FY2028'), 'multiplier', '>'),\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:662:        ('T4', src('t', 'e3660', 'more than double', 2.0, 'x', 'guidance', W('x', 'x', 'x', 'x'), 'multiplier', '>'), True),\n184:def check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n476:    g24x = copy.deepcopy(c24)                         # [46o.1] q01's base added and subtracted: it cancels out\n479:    item('G24x', f24, G_PASSAGES, g24x, lambda r: (any(c == 'C9' and 'does not change' in d for c, d in r['errors']), 'C9: a base that cancels'), '')\n484:    f4x, c4x = g4()                                   # [46p] a same-period cancellation on a range base: $554B + x - x\n485:    c4x['figures'].append(comp('g28cancel', 'sum', ['gc554', 'gfy28c', 'gfy28c'], 554.0, 'USD bn', W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', CO, 'FY2028'),\n487:    next(f for f in c4x['figures'] if f['id'] == 'gfy28')['rests_on'] = ['g28cancel', 'i256']\n488:    item('G4x', f4x, G_PASSAGES, c4x, lambda r: (any(c == 'C9' and 'g28cancel' in d and 'does not change' in d for c, d in r['errors'])\n489:                                                 and 'C8' not in codes(r), 'C9: a range base that cancels, no C8 mask'), '')\n556:    f4y, c4y = g4()                                   # two distinct figures with one derivation: a - b cancels, netting cannot see it\n558:    c4y['figures'].append(comp('g28cancel', 'sum', ['gc554', 'gfy28c', 'gfy28d'], 554.0, 'USD bn', W('NVIDIA', 'revenue', CO, 'FY2028'),\n560:    next(f for f in c4y['figures'] if f['id'] == 'gfy28')['rests_on'] = ['g28cancel', 'i256']\n561:    item('G4x2', f4y, G_PASSAGES, c4y, lambda r: (any(c == 'C9' and 'g28cancel' in d and 'does not change' in d for c, d in r['errors']),\n562:                                                   'C9: two copies of one derivation cancel'), '')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
